What is High Density Semiconductor Memory?
High density semiconductor memory is engineered to pack a maximal amount of storage cells within a limited chip area. By leveraging sub‑10 nm process nodes, three‑dimensional stacking, and advanced cell architectures such as multi‑level and triple‑level cells, manufacturers can achieve terabit‑scale capacities on a single die. The technology serves a broad spectrum of applications, from hyperscale data‑center servers that require petabyte‑scale storage to compact edge devices that must run AI inference locally while meeting stringent power budgets.
